{"id": "c9702_v1_u0_c24_pdf390_1114", "book_title": "Cambridge International AS and A Level Physics Coursebook (2nd edition)", "author": "David Sang, Graham Jones, Gurinder Chadha, Richard Woodside", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": 24, "chapter_title": "Capacitance", "section": "Q₁ = C₁V   and   Q₂ = C₂V Capacitors in series", "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [378, 378], "pdf_pages": [390, 390], "text": "Q₁ = C₁V   and   Q₂ = C₂V Capacitors in series\n\nC₁ Q₁ In a similar way to the case of capacitors connected in\n\nparallel, we can consider two or more capacitors connected in series (Figure 24.12). The total capacitance Ctotal of two capacitors of capacitances C₁ and C₂ is given by: 1 1 1 =  + Ctotal C₁ C₂ V Here, it is the reciprocals of the capacitances that must be 378 added to give the reciprocal of the total capacitance. For three or more capacitors connected in series, the equation for their total capacitance is: 1 1 1 1 C₂ Q₂ =  +  +  + … Ctotal C₁ C₂ C₃ Figure 24.11  Two capacitors connected in parallel have the same p.d. across them, but different amounts of charge. C₁ C₂ The total charge is given by the sum of these: Figure 24.12  Two capacitors connected in series.\n\nQ = Q₁ + Q₂ = C₁V + C₂V", "char_count": 809}
