{"id": "c9702_v1_u0_c24_pdf390_1116", "book_title": "Cambridge International AS and A Level Physics Coursebook (2nd edition)", "author": "David Sang, Graham Jones, Gurinder Chadha, Richard Woodside", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": 24, "chapter_title": "Capacitance", "section": null, "content_type": "body", "book_pages": [378, 378], "pdf_pages": [390, 390], "text": "Capacitors in parallel: summary V = V₁ + V₂\n\nFor capacitors in parallel, the following rules apply:\n\nC₁ C₂ The p.d. across each capacitor is the same. –Q +Q –Q +Q The total charge on the capacitors is equal to the sum of the V₁ V₂ charges: Qtotal = Q₁ +  Q₂ +  Q₃ + … V The total capacitance Ctotal is given by: Figure 24.13  Capacitors connected in series store the same\n\nCtotal =  C₁ +  C₂ + C₃ + … charge, but they have different p.d.s across them.", "char_count": 451}
