{"id": "c9702_v1_u0_c0_pdf596_1708", "book_title": "Cambridge International AS and A Level Physics Coursebook (2nd edition)", "author": "David Sang, Graham Jones, Gurinder Chadha, Richard Woodside", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Answers to SAQs", "section": null, "content_type": "answers_saqs", "book_pages": [584, 584], "pdf_pages": [596, 596], "text": "c\t No, because the stone remains at a constant\n\ndistance from the centre of the circle. d\t No, because you do not move.\n\n2\tWork done, W = F × s = mg × s\n\n= 70 × 9.81 × 2.5 = 1720 ≈ 1700 J\n\n3\ta\t Work done, W = F × s = 10 × 250 = 2500 J\n\nb\t 2500 J (ignoring work done against air\n\nresistance)", "char_count": 290}
{"id": "c9702_v1_u0_c0_pdf596_1709", "book_title": "Cambridge International AS and A Level Physics Coursebook (2nd edition)", "author": "David Sang, Graham Jones, Gurinder Chadha, Richard Woodside", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Answers to SAQs", "section": null, "content_type": "answers_saqs", "book_pages": [584, 584], "pdf_pages": [596, 596], "text": "4\tThe crane does work to overcome the\n\ndownward force of gravity, so all that matters is the vertical height through which the crane lifts the load. Work done, W \u0007= F × s = 500 × 40 = 20 000 J = 20 kJ 5\tWork done by force up slope,\n\nW = F × s = 100 × 0.50 = 50 J Work done by contact force = 0 J Work done by force down slope, W = F × s = – (30 × 0.50) = –15 J (note the movement is in the opposite direction to the force) Work done by gravity, W = F × s = –(100 sin 45° × 0.50) = –35 J\n\n6\tWork done, W = p∆v = 1.0 × 10⁵ × 0.002 = 200 J\n\n7\tChange in gravitational potential energy,\n\nEp = mgh = 52 × 9.81 × 2.5 = 1275 J ≈ 1300 J 8\tChange in gravitational potential energy,\n\nEp \u0007= mgh = 100 × 9.81 × 5500 = 5400 kJ or 5.4 MJ 9\ta\t Elastic potential energy.\n\nb\t Work is done on the magnets in pulling them\n\napart. The potential energy transferred to the\n\n10\tKinetic energy of car, 1² 1 Ek = mv =  × 500 × (15)² = 56 kJ 2 2 Kinetic energy of motorcycle, 1² 1 Ek = mv =  × 250 × (30)² = 156 kJ 2 2 The motorcycle has more k.e.\n\n11\tChange in kinetic energy\n\n= k.e. before – k.e. after: 1² 1 =  × 0.200 × (15.8) –  × 0.200 × (12.2)² 2 2 = 25 – 15 = 10 J", "char_count": 1145}
{"id": "c9702_v1_u0_c0_pdf596_1710", "book_title": "Cambridge International AS and A Level Physics Coursebook (2nd edition)", "author": "David Sang, Graham Jones, Gurinder Chadha, Richard Woodside", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Answers to SAQs", "section": null, "content_type": "answers_saqs", "book_pages": [584, 584], "pdf_pages": [596, 596], "text": "12\tCalculate the loss in g.p.e. as the sphere falls\n\nfrom its highest position. Ep = mgh = 10.0 × 9.81 × 0.15 = 14.7 J The gain in the sphere’s k.e. is 14.7 J. Using this to calculate the sphere’s speed, 1² 1 Ek = mv =  × 10.0 × v² = 14.7 J 2 2 14.7 So v² =  = 2.94 m² s–² and v = 1.7 m s–¹, 5.0 the same as for the sphere of mass 5.0 kg; the result is unchanged for any value of mass. 13\tReduction in gravitational potential energy,\n\n∆Ep \u0007= mg∆h = 80 000 × 9.81 × (10 000 – 1000) = 7.1 × 10⁹ J This energy becomes increased kinetic energy of air molecules – the air temperature rises.", "char_count": 585}
{"id": "c9702_v1_u0_c0_pdf596_1711", "book_title": "Cambridge International AS and A Level Physics Coursebook (2nd edition)", "author": "David Sang, Graham Jones, Gurinder Chadha, Richard Woodside", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Answers to SAQs", "section": null, "content_type": "answers_saqs", "book_pages": [584, 584], "pdf_pages": [596, 596], "text": "14\tWe can say that the change in g.p.e. from the\n\ntop of the jump to the point just before she enters the water is equal to the change in 1² kinetic energy, so that: mgh = mv 2 This means that her speed just before the point where she enters the water is v =   2gh =    2 × 9.81 × 10 =    196 = 14 m s–¹\n\n15\ta\t Kinetic energy as stone reaches foot of cliff,\n\n1² 1 Ek = mv =  × m × (38)² = m × 722 J 2 2 Gravitational potential energy change, Ep = mgh = m × 9.81 × 80 = m × 785 J So proportion of g.p.e. converted to k.e. k.e. 722 =  = 0.92 = 92% g.p.e. 785\n\nb\t The rest of the stone’s initial energy is", "char_count": 602}
{"id": "c9702_v1_u0_c0_pdf597_1712", "book_title": "Cambridge International AS and A Level Physics Coursebook (2nd edition)", "author": "David Sang, Graham Jones, Gurinder Chadha, Richard Woodside", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Answers to SAQs", "section": null, "content_type": "answers_saqs", "book_pages": [585, 585], "pdf_pages": [597, 597], "text": "work done\n\n16\tRearranging power =  gives time taken work done = power × time taken = 50 000 W × 60 s = 3.0 × 10⁶ J (or 3.0 MJ) W 4 200 000\n\n17\tPower =  =  = 70 000 W (or 70 kW) t 60 18\ta\t Work done in one second,\n\nW = Fs = 700 × 40 = 28 000 J (28 kJ)\n\nb\t 28 kW\n\n19\tWork done against gravity,\n\nW \u0007= Fs = weight × height moved = mgh = 55 × 9.81 × 28 × 0.20 = 3020 J W 3020 So her useful power =  =  = 560 W t 5.4", "char_count": 410}
{"id": "c9702_v1_u0_c0_pdf598_1713", "book_title": "Cambridge International AS and A Level Physics Coursebook (2nd edition)", "author": "David Sang, Graham Jones, Gurinder Chadha, Richard Woodside", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Answers to SAQs", "section": "Chapter 6", "content_type": "answers_saqs", "book_pages": [586, 586], "pdf_pages": [598, 598], "text": "Chapter 6\n\n1\ta\t Ball B has greater mass.\n\nb\t Trolley B has greater mass.\n\n2\ta\t Momentum, p = mv\n\nb\t Momentum, p \u0007= mv = 25 000 × 20\n\n= 5.0 × 10⁵ kg m s\n\nc\t Momentum, p \u0007= mv = 9.1 × 10\n\n= 1.82 × 10–²³ ≈ 1.8 × 10–²³ kg m s", "char_count": 221}
{"id": "c9702_v1_u0_c0_pdf598_1714", "book_title": "Cambridge International AS and A Level Physics Coursebook (2nd edition)", "author": "David Sang, Graham Jones, Gurinder Chadha, Richard Woodside", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Answers to SAQs", "section": null, "content_type": "answers_saqs", "book_pages": [586, 586], "pdf_pages": [598, 598], "text": "3\tMomentum before\n\n= mA uA + mB uB = (0.50 × 2.0) + (0.50 × (–3.0)) = 1.0 – 1.5 = –0.5 kg m s–¹ Momentum after = mA vA + mB = (0.50 × (–2.0)) + (0.50 × 1.0) = –1.0 + 0.5 = –0.5 kg m s–¹ (i.e. to the left)", "char_count": 204}
{"id": "c9702_v1_u0_c0_pdf598_1715", "book_title": "Cambridge International AS and A Level Physics Coursebook (2nd edition)", "author": "David Sang, Graham Jones, Gurinder Chadha, Richard Woodside", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Answers to SAQs", "section": null, "content_type": "answers_saqs", "book_pages": [586, 586], "pdf_pages": [598, 598], "text": "4\n\nType of collision perfectly elastic inelastic Momentum conserved Kinetic energy conserved Total energy conserved 5\ta\t Before collision:\n\nmomentum of ball A, pA = mA uA = 4.0 × 2.5 = +10 kg m s momentum of ball B, pB = mB uB = 4.0 × (–1.5) = –6 kg m s b\t After collision:\n\nmomentum of ball A, pA = mA uA = 4.0 × (–1.5) = –6 kg m s momentum of ball B, pB = mB uB = 4.0 × 2.5 = +10 kg m s\n\nc\t Total momentum before collision\n\n= pA + pB = +4 kg m s–¹ Total momentum after collision = pA + pB = +4 kg m s–¹ So momentum is conserved.\n\nd\t Kinetic energy before collision\n\n1² 1² = m u + m u 2A A 2B B 1² 1 = ( × 4.0 × (2.5) ) + ( × 4.0 × (1.5) 2 2\n\nKinetic energy after collision 1² 1² = m v + m v 2A A 2B B 1² 1 = ( × 4.0 × (1.5) ) + ( × 4.0 × (2.5)²) 2 2 = 4.5 + 12.5 = 17 J So k.e. before = k.e. after. = 0.50 × 20 = 10 kg m s–¹\n\ne\t Relative speed before = 2.5 – (–1.5) = 4.0 m s–¹ Relative speed after = 2.5 – (–1.5) = 4.0 m s–¹", "char_count": 927}
{"id": "c9702_v1_u0_c0_pdf598_1716", "book_title": "Cambridge International AS and A Level Physics Coursebook (2nd edition)", "author": "David Sang, Graham Jones, Gurinder Chadha, Richard Woodside", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Answers to SAQs", "section": null, "content_type": "answers_saqs", "book_pages": [586, 586], "pdf_pages": [598, 598], "text": "–³¹ × 2.0 × 10⁷ 6\ta\n\n(i.e. to the left)\n\nvB b\t Call 1.0 kg trolley A. Call 2.0 kg trolley B. Conservation of momentum means: momentum before collision = momentum after collision conserved So mA uA + mB uB = mA vA + mB vB not conserved Rearrange to find speed of first trolley after conserved collision, vA. mA uA + mB uB – mB vB = mA vA m u + m u – m v vA\t= A AB BB B mA = (1.0 × 2.0) + (2.0 × 0.0) – (2.0 × 1.2) 1.0 = –0.40 m s–¹ The minus sign indicates that the first trolley reverses direction.", "char_count": 498}
{"id": "c9702_v1_u0_c0_pdf598_1717", "book_title": "Cambridge International AS and A Level Physics Coursebook (2nd edition)", "author": "David Sang, Graham Jones, Gurinder Chadha, Richard Woodside", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Answers to SAQs", "section": null, "content_type": "answers_saqs", "book_pages": [586, 586], "pdf_pages": [598, 598], "text": "7\ta\t If you consider the star to be stationary before\n\nexploding, the star has zero momentum. After the explosion, matter flies off in all directions – equal amounts of momentum are created in all directions, so their (vector) sum is zero. Momentum is conserved. b\t You give downward momentum to the Earth;\n\nas you slow down, so does the Earth; as you start to fall back down, the Earth starts to ‘fall’ back up towards you. At all times, your momentum is equal and opposite to that of ²) the Earth, so combined momentum is zero,", "char_count": 529}
