{"id": "c9702_v1_u0_c0_pdf601_1724", "book_title": "Cambridge International AS and A Level Physics Coursebook (2nd edition)", "author": "David Sang, Graham Jones, Gurinder Chadha, Richard Woodside", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Answers to SAQs", "section": null, "content_type": "answers_saqs", "book_pages": [589, 589], "pdf_pages": [601, 601], "text": "Metals from stiffest to least stiff: Metal Young modulus / GPa Most stiff steel 210 iron (wrought) 200 copper 130 brass 90–110 aluminium 70 tin 50 Least stiff lead 18\n\nStiffest non-metal is glass (Young modulus\n\n= 70–80 GPa). For material A, Young modulus\n\nstress σ 15 × 10⁶ EA =  =  =  = 1.5 × 10¹⁰ Pa = 1.5 GPa strain ε 0.001 For material B, Young modulus, stress σ 12 × 10⁶ EB =  =  =  = 5.0 × 10⁹ Pa = 5.0 GPa strain ε 0.0024 force\n\nStress = cross-sectional area 50 =  = 1.0 × 10⁸ Pa 0.5 × 10–⁶ (remember that 0.5 mm² = 0.5 × 10–⁶ m) extension 0.1 Strain =  = original length 200.0 = 5.0 × 10–⁴ (0.05%) stress 1.0 × 10⁸ Young modulus\t=  = –₄ strain 5.0 × 10 = 2.0 × 10¹¹ Pa stress\n\nYoung modulus, E = strain stress Rearrange so strain = Young modulus Then insert formulae for stress and strain x F = L A × E πd² and cross-sectional area, A = 4 4FL This gives extension\t= ₂ πd × E 4 × 10 × 1.00 = π × (0.001)² × 130 × 10⁹ = 9.796 × 10–⁵ m ≈ 9.8 × 10–⁵ m force 4F\n\nStress\t=  = ₂ cross-sectional area πd 4 × 1.00 = ₂ = 8.0 × 10⁶ Pa π × (0.0004)", "char_count": 1045}
{"id": "c9702_v1_u0_c0_pdf602_1725", "book_title": "Cambridge International AS and A Level Physics Coursebook (2nd edition)", "author": "David Sang, Graham Jones, Gurinder Chadha, Richard Woodside", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Answers to SAQs", "section": null, "content_type": "answers_saqs", "book_pages": [590, 590], "pdf_pages": [602, 602], "text": "stress 8.0 × 10⁶\n\nYoung modulus =  = –₃ strain 1.25 × 10 = 6.4 × 10⁹ Pa (but could be more, because extension may be less than 1 mm). stress 150 × 10⁶\n\n14\ta\t Young modulus =   =  = 50 GPa, strain 0.003 breaking stress = 150 MPa stress 100 × 10⁶ b\t Young modulus =  =  = 100 GPa, strain 0.001 breaking stress = 130 MPa Note that the Young modulus is only found for the straight portion of the stress–strain graph. stress 100 × 10⁶ c\t Young modulus =  =  = 25 GPa, strain 0.004 breaking stress = 100 MPa 15\tElastic potential energy,\n\n1 1 E = Fx =   × 12 × 0.18 = 1.08 J ≈ 1.1 J 2 2 The rubber band is assumed to obey Hooke’s law; hence the answer is an estimate. 1 1²\n\n16\tElastic potential energy, E = Fx = kx 2 2 1 =   × 4800 × (0.0020)² = 9.6 × 10–³ J 2\n\n17\ta\t A has greater stiffness (less extension per unit\n\nforce).\n\nb\t A requires greater force to break (line\n\ncontinues to higher force value).\n\nc\t B requires greater amount of work done to\n\nbreak (larger area under graph).", "char_count": 977}
