{"id": "c9702_v1_u0_c0_pdf604_1729", "book_title": "Cambridge International AS and A Level Physics Coursebook (2nd edition)", "author": "David Sang, Graham Jones, Gurinder Chadha, Richard Woodside", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Answers to SAQs", "section": null, "content_type": "answers_saqs", "book_pages": [592, 592], "pdf_pages": [604, 604], "text": "6\ta\t Rearrange ΔQ = IΔt and use 50 A per hour as\n\nthe charge available, so ΔQ 50 Δt = =  = 0.25 hours = 15 minutes I 200 b\t ΔQ = IΔt; for a current of 200 A, ΔQ = 200 × 15 × 60 = 180 000 C\n\n7\tNumber of protons in 1 C\n\n1 =  = 6.2 × 10¹⁸ protons 1.6 × 10–¹⁹\n\n8\tThe smallest possible unit of electric charge\n\nis the elementary charge, 1.6 × 10–¹⁹ C. So the only possible electrical charges are whole multiples of this elementary charge. The only value from the list that is a whole multiple is 8.0 × 10–¹⁹ C.\n\n9\tCurrent I = nAve\n\n= 5.9 × 10²⁸ × 2.0 × 10–⁶ × 0.1 × 10–³ × 1.6 × 10–¹⁹ = 1.9 A\n\n10\tCross-sectional area of copper wire\n\nπd² π × (1.0 × 10–³)² A = πr² =  =  = 0.79 × 10–⁶ m² 4 4 Rearrange I = nAve to give drift velocity I 5.0 v\t=  = nAe 8.5 × 10²⁸ × 0.79 × 10–⁶ × 1.6 × 10–¹⁹ = 0.47 × 10–³ m s–¹ = 0.47 mm s–¹", "char_count": 817}
{"id": "c9702_v1_u0_c0_pdf604_1730", "book_title": "Cambridge International AS and A Level Physics Coursebook (2nd edition)", "author": "David Sang, Graham Jones, Gurinder Chadha, Richard Woodside", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Answers to SAQs", "section": null, "content_type": "answers_saqs", "book_pages": [592, 592], "pdf_pages": [604, 604], "text": "11\tCurrent I = nAve, but remember that, in a series circuit, current is the same all the way round the circuit. Also, A and e are the same for both lengths of wire. This means that n₁Av₁e = n₂Av₂e, where the 1 denotes the copper wire and the 2 denotes the silver wire. V² n¹ 8.5 × 10²⁸ So that means  =  =  = 1.4. V₁ n₂ 5.9 × 10²⁸ This means that the drift velocity in the silver wire is 1.4 times the drift velocity in the copper wire. V 12\n\n12\tCurrent I =  =  = 0.33 A R 36\n\n13\tA 60 W lamp has higher resistance. It allows\n\nless current through for the same potential difference (power is current × potential difference, so, applying Ohm’s law, power is inversely proportional to resistance).\n\n14\ta\t Potential difference, V = IR = 1.0 × 50 = 50 V\n\nb\t Potential difference, V = IR = 2.0 × 50 = 100 V\n\nV 230\n\n15\tResistance R =  =  = 580 Ω (to 2 sig. figs) I 0.40 V 6.0\n\n16\tResistance R =  =  = 2.5 Ω I 2.4 17\tPower P = VI, so rearrange to give current:\n\nP 60 I =  =  = 0.26 A V 230\n\n18\tPower P = VI = 25 × 10³ × 40 × 10³ = 1.0 × 10⁹ W\n\n(1000 MW or 1 GW) 19\ta\t Power P = VI so rearrange to give current:\n\nP 10⁴ I =  =  = 43 A V 230 b\t Fuse needs to be rated above, but close to, full normal current draw, so a fuse of 45 to 50 A would be appropriate. V² (3.0)²\n\n20\tPower P =  =  = 0.45 mW R (20 × 10³) P 15\n\n21\ta\t Current I =  =  = 0.065 A V 230 V 230\n\nb\t Resistance R =  =  = 3500 Ω I 0.065 22\tRearrange power P = I²R to give resistance:\n\nP 100", "char_count": 1444}
{"id": "c9702_v1_u0_c0_pdf605_1731", "book_title": "Cambridge International AS and A Level Physics Coursebook (2nd edition)", "author": "David Sang, Graham Jones, Gurinder Chadha, Richard Woodside", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Answers to SAQs", "section": null, "content_type": "answers_saqs", "book_pages": [593, 593], "pdf_pages": [605, 605], "text": "23\tEnergy transferred\n\nW \u0007= IVΔt = 10 × 12 × (5.0 × 60 × 60) = 2200 000 J, or 2.2 MJ\n\n24\ta\t Charge through lamp\n\nΔQ = IΔt = 10 × 20 = 200 C b\t Per coulomb of charge,\n\nW 400 energy =  =  = 2.0 J C–¹ ΔQ 200 c\t Rearrange energy transferred, W = IVΔt, ΔW to give potential difference: V\t= IΔt 400 =  = 2.0 V (10 × 20) energy or use potential difference\t= charge 400 =  = 2.0 V 200", "char_count": 376}
{"id": "c9702_v1_u0_c0_pdf606_1732", "book_title": "Cambridge International AS and A Level Physics Coursebook (2nd edition)", "author": "David Sang, Graham Jones, Gurinder Chadha, Richard Woodside", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Answers to SAQs", "section": "Chapter 10", "content_type": "answers_saqs", "book_pages": [594, 594], "pdf_pages": [606, 606], "text": "Chapter 10\n\n1\tKirchhoff’s first law means that\n\nI + 3.0 A = 7.5 A, so I = 7.5 – 3.0 = 4.5 A", "char_count": 91}
{"id": "c9702_v1_u0_c0_pdf606_1733", "book_title": "Cambridge International AS and A Level Physics Coursebook (2nd edition)", "author": "David Sang, Graham Jones, Gurinder Chadha, Richard Woodside", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Answers to SAQs", "section": null, "content_type": "answers_saqs", "book_pages": [594, 594], "pdf_pages": [606, 606], "text": "2\tCurrent flowing into P equals current flowing\n\nout of P, so if current in wire X is Ix, then Ix + 3.0 A + 2.5 A = 7.0 A So Ix = 7.0 – 3.0 – 2.5 = 1.5 A, towards P\n\n3\tCurrent flowing into junction\n\nΣIiₙ = 3.0 + 2.5 + 1.0 = 6.5 A Current flowing out of junction ΣIout = 4.0 + 0.5 + 2.0 = 6.5 A Yes, Kirchhoff’s law is satisfied.\n\n4\tIx = 3.0 + 2.0 – 7.0 = –2.0 A, which means the\n\ncurrent is towards P.\n\n5\tSum of e.m.f.s around any loop in a circuit is\n\nequal to the sum of the p.d.s around the loop. So e.m.f. of power supply = Σp.d.s across resistors, meaning p.d. across resistor R = e.m.f. of  – p.d. across power supply 20 Ω resistor = 10 – (0.1 × 20) = 8.0 V V 8.0 V = IR so resistance R =  =  = 80 Ω I 0.1\n\n6\ta\t Choose the loop containing the 5 V cell at the\n\ntop, the 10 Ω resistor with current I, and the central 5 V cell, as the only current involved is I.\n\nb\t Sum of e.m.f.s of cells in loop = 5.0 + 5.0 = 10 V\n\n= p.d. across resistor. V 10 V = IR so I =  =  = 1.0 A R 10\n\n7\tIn loop, sum of p.d.f.s = 30 – 10 = 20 V, which\n\nby Kirchhoff’s second law must equal the sum of the p.d.s across the resistors, given by V = IR. Sum of p.d.s across resistors = (0.5 × R) + (0.5 × 10) + (0.2 × 10) + (0.2 × 20) So 20 = (0.5 × R) + 11, giving (20 – 11) R =  = 18 Ω 0.5\n\n8\tIn series, the 1 C charge passes through both\n\nbatteries and gains or loses 6 J in each. If the", "char_count": 1367}
{"id": "c9702_v1_u0_c0_pdf606_1734", "book_title": "Cambridge International AS and A Level Physics Coursebook (2nd edition)", "author": "David Sang, Graham Jones, Gurinder Chadha, Richard Woodside", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Answers to SAQs", "section": null, "content_type": "answers_saqs", "book_pages": [594, 594], "pdf_pages": [606, 606], "text": "move the charge in the same direction, the total e.m.f. = 6 + 6 = 12 V. If the batteries are connected back to front, the charge gains energy in one cell but loses it in the other, so total e.m.f. = 0 V. In parallel, half the charge flows through one battery and half through the other, so the total energy gained is 6 J, meaning the total e.m.f. = 6 V. 9\tConsider the circuit loop at the top,\n\ncontaining the 10 V cell and a 20 Ω resistor. Use Kirchhoff’s second law and V = IR to give 10 V = I₁ × 20 Ω, so current through A₁ is 10 I₁ =  = 0.50 A 20 Consider the circuit loop at the bottom, containing the 5 V cell and a 20 Ω resistor. Use Kirchhoff’s second law and V = IR to give 5 V = I₁ × 20 Ω, so current through A₃ is 5 I₃ =  = 0.25 A 20 Now use Kirchhoff’s first law at the circuit junction to the right of ammeter A₂ to give I₁ = I₂ + I₃ So current through A₂ is I₂ = I₁ – I₃ = 0.50 – 0.25 A\n\n10\tTotal resistance = 5 + 5 + 10 = 20 Ω\n\n11\tUse Kirchhoff’s second law to give\n\ne.m.f. = V₁ + V₂ So V₂ = e.m.f. – V₁ = 2.0 – 1.2 = 0.8 V\n\n12\ta\t All five in series and pointing the same way, so\n\ne.m.f. = 1.5 + 1.5 + 1.5 + 1.5 + 1.5 = 7.5 V\n\nb\t Five in series, with two facing in the opposite\n\ndirection, so e.m.f. = 1.5 + 1.5 + 1.5 – 1.5 – 1.5 = 1.5 V\n\nor all five in parallel\n\nc\t Five in series, with one facing in the opposite\n\ndirection, so e.m.f. = 1.5 + 1.5 + 1.5 + 1.5 – 1.5 = 4.5 V or two in parallel to give e.m.f. of 1.5 V, connected in series to two more in parallel (also giving e.m.f. of 1.5 V), then connected in series", "char_count": 1533}
