{"id": "c9702_v1_u0_c0_pdf614_1754", "book_title": "Cambridge International AS and A Level Physics Coursebook (2nd edition)", "author": "David Sang, Graham Jones, Gurinder Chadha, Richard Woodside", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Answers to SAQs", "section": null, "content_type": "answers_saqs", "book_pages": [602, 602], "pdf_pages": [614, 614], "text": "1\ta\t Wavelength of stationary wave = 2 × distance between nodes = 50 cm Subtract the first equation from the second to give λ/2 = l₂ – l₁ , meaning\n\nb\t Distance from node to adjacent antinode λ = 2 × (0.388 – 0.126) = 0.524 m ≈ 52 cm = 0.5 × distance between nodes = 12.5 cm\n\nb\t For the shorter tube, the end correction\n\n2\ta\t One complete wavelength is shown in the λ 52.4 c =  – l =  = 0.50 cm 4¹ 4 – 12.6 image, so wavelength = 60 cm\n\nc\t Speed v = fλ = 630 × 0.524 = 330 m s–¹ Separation of two antinodes = λ/2 = 30 cm\n\nb\n\nc\t Length of string = 60 cm, so to produce three\n\nantinodes, 3λ/2 = 60 cm, meaning λ = 40 cm\n\n3\ta\tnodes\n\ntransmitter reflector\n\nantinodes\n\nb\t Distance between antinodes = 14 mm = λ/2,\n\nso wavelength λ = 28 mm. Frequency v 3.0 × 10⁸ f =  =  = 1.07 × 10¹⁰ Hz ≈ 11 GHz λ 0.028\n\n4\tIn both cases, waves are reflected (by the\n\nmetal sheet or by the water). The outgoing and reflected waves combine to produce a stationary wave pattern.\n\n5\ta\t It is much easier to detect where sound falls\n\nto zero than where sound is a maximum.\n\nb\t Increased accuracy – if the wavelength\n\nis short, it is difficult to measure just one wavelength.\n\n6\ta\t Three antinodes between them means the\n\ndistance between the two measured nodes = 3λ/2 = 20 cm, so wavelength λ = 13.3 cm ≈ 13 cm\n\nb\t Speed v = fλ = 2500 × 0.13 = 325 m s–¹ ≈ 330 m s–¹", "char_count": 1339}
