{"id": "c9702_v1_u0_c0_pdf629_1796", "book_title": "Cambridge International AS and A Level Physics Coursebook (2nd edition)", "author": "David Sang, Graham Jones, Gurinder Chadha, Richard Woodside", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Answers to SAQs", "section": null, "content_type": "answers_saqs", "book_pages": [617, 617], "pdf_pages": [629, 629], "text": "a\t Work done in uniform field = QV so\n\nE → H, 5 kJ; E → F, 2.5 kJ; F → G, 0 J; H → E, –5 kJ b\t i\t E → H, –5 kJ; E → F, –2.5 kJ; F → G, 0 J;\n\nH → E, +5 kJ ii\t E → H, 10 kJ; E → F, 5 kJ; F → G, 0 J;\n\nH → E, –10 kJ Q Q\n\nElectrostatic force FE = ¹ ²₂ 4πε₀r (+1.6 × 10 )² = –₁₂–¹⁹–₁₂ = 230 N 4 × π × 8.85 × 10 × (1.0 × 10 5) GMm Gravitational force FG = – ₂ r 6.67 × 10–¹¹ × (1.67 × 10–²⁷)² =  = 1.9 × 10–³⁴ N (1.0 × 10–¹⁵)² This answer tells us that the gravitational attraction is nowhere near enough to balance the electric repulsion. Some other force must hold the protons together. (In fact, it is the strong nuclear force.)", "char_count": 624}
