{"id": "c9702_v1_u0_c0_pdf643_1830", "book_title": "Cambridge International AS and A Level Physics Coursebook (2nd edition)", "author": "David Sang, Graham Jones, Gurinder Chadha, Richard Woodside", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Answers to SAQs", "section": null, "content_type": "answers_saqs", "book_pages": [631, 631], "pdf_pages": [643, 643], "text": "21\ta\t We need to find an expression for the decay\n\nconstant λ so that we can substitute it into the decay equation. The quantity f is the ratio of atoms remaining to decay to the original number of atoms in N the sample: f = N₀ 1–λt When t = t₁/₂, f =  = e₁/₂ 2 1 Taking logarithms of both sides, ln( ) = –λt 2¹/² ln(¹) Therefore λ = – ² t₁/₂ Then the equation f = e–λt becomes f = elⁿ⁽¹²⁾t₁/₂t 1", "char_count": 396}
{"id": "c9702_v1_u0_c0_pdf644_1831", "book_title": "Cambridge International AS and A Level Physics Coursebook (2nd edition)", "author": "David Sang, Graham Jones, Gurinder Chadha, Richard Woodside", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Answers to SAQs", "section": null, "content_type": "answers_saqs", "book_pages": [632, 632], "pdf_pages": [644, 644], "text": "Chapter 32\n\n1\ta\t Electrical energy from power supply is\n\ntransferred to energy of electron beam (100%). Energy of electron beam is transferred to internal energy of the anode (~99%) and energy of X-ray photons (~1%).\n\nb\t Energy \u0007= qV = 80 keV = 80 000 × 1.6 × 10–¹⁹\n\n= 1.28 × 10–¹⁴ J ≈ 1.3 × 10–¹⁴ J Impact speed is given by rearranging kinetic 1² energy Ek = mv to give 2 2E 2 × 1.3 × 10–¹⁴ v =    k =    –₃₁ = 1.69 × 10⁸ m s–¹ m 9.1 × 10 ≈ 1.7 × 10⁸ m s–¹ hc\n\n2\tPhoton energy E =  = V × e λ hc 6.63 × 10–³⁴ × 3.0 × 10⁸ Wavelength λ\t=  = Ve 120 × 10³ × 1.6 × 10–¹⁹ = 1.04 × 10–¹¹ m ≈ 1.0 × 10–¹¹ m\n\n3\tWhen x = x₁/₂ the intensity has dropped to\n\nhalf its initial value. Hence I = I₀ e–μx becomes\n\nI–μx = I e¹/². Taking logs of both sides gives 2⁰ ln(½) = –μx₁/₂ or ln 2 = μx₁/₂. Rearranging gives x₁/₂ = ln(2/μ). P 400\n\n4\tIntensity =  =  = 8.0 × 10⁵ W m–² A 0.0005\n\n5\tIntensity I = I₀ e–μx = 50 × e⁽–¹.² × ⁵.⁰⁾ = 0.12 W m–²\n\n6\tThe grid absorbs scattered X-rays, which\n\nwould otherwise cloud the image.", "char_count": 1001}
{"id": "c9702_v1_u0_c0_pdf644_1832", "book_title": "Cambridge International AS and A Level Physics Coursebook (2nd edition)", "author": "David Sang, Graham Jones, Gurinder Chadha, Richard Woodside", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Answers to SAQs", "section": null, "content_type": "answers_saqs", "book_pages": [632, 632], "pdf_pages": [644, 644], "text": "7\tConsider the ratio of attenuation coefficients\n\nbone : muscle. This is approximately 6 for 50 keV X-rays, so bone is a much better absorber at this energy than is muscle. At 4.0 MeV, the ratio is less than 2, so bone and muscle will not appear very different on the image. (You could also calculate the fraction of X-rays absorbed by, say, 1 cm of tissue. At 4.0 MeV, only a small fraction is absorbed, so the X-ray image will be flooded with unabsorbed X-rays.) Z\n\n9\tBreathing causes movement of the body so that organs or bones of interest may move in the X-ray beam as the image is processed.\n\n10\tThe skull has bone all round. In a\n\nconventional X-ray, the beam must pass through both sides of the skull and this makes it difficult to see the inner tissue. In a CAT scan, the inner tissue shows up more clearly and any damage to the skull bones can be pinpointed accurately.\n\n11\ta\t Rearrange speed v = fλ to give wavelength\n\nv 5700 λ =  =  = 2.7 × 10–³ m (2.7 mm) f 2.1 × 10⁶\n\nb\t 1.35 × 10–³ m ≈ 1.4 mm\n\n12\ta\t Mechanical to electrical\n\nb\t Electrical to mechanical\n\nc\t Mechanical to electrical\n\nd\t Electrical to mechanical\n\n13\tZ = ρc = 1075 × 1590 = 1.71 × 10⁶ kg m² s–¹\n\nIr (Z – Z )² (1.34 – 1.50)²\n\n14\t = ²¹₂ = ₂ = 3.2 × 10–³ (0.32%) I₀ (Z₂ + Z₁) (1.34 + 1.50)\n\n15\tThere is a big change in acoustic impedance\n\nwhen ultrasound passes from fluid into skin, and from tissue into bone. These surfaces therefore give strong reflections. Other soft tissues have similar values of acoustic impedance and so reflections are very weak.", "char_count": 1532}
{"id": "c9702_v1_u0_c0_pdf644_1833", "book_title": "Cambridge International AS and A Level Physics Coursebook (2nd edition)", "author": "David Sang, Graham Jones, Gurinder Chadha, Richard Woodside", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Answers to SAQs", "section": null, "content_type": "answers_saqs", "book_pages": [632, 632], "pdf_pages": [644, 644], "text": "16\tThe brain is surrounded by solid bone which\n\nreflects ultrasound. Little penetrates the brain and hence the signal is very weak. An alternative is a CT scan. cΔt 1540 × 0.034 × 10–³\n\n17\tDistance\t=  = 2 2 = 0.026 m (26 mm)\n\n18\tX-rays are ionising radiation and hence are\n\ndamaging to the fetus. Ultrasound carries very little risk because it is not a form of ionising radiation. (The intensity used must not cause heating of the baby’s tissues.)", "char_count": 447}
