{"id": "c9702_v1_u0_c0_pdf663_1880", "book_title": "Cambridge International AS and A Level Physics Coursebook (2nd edition)", "author": "David Sang, Graham Jones, Gurinder Chadha, Richard Woodside", "unit_no": null, "unit_name": null, "chapter_no": null, "chapter_title": "Answers to EOC questions", "section": null, "content_type": "answers_eoc", "book_pages": [651, 651], "pdf_pages": [663, 663], "text": "b\t In a metallic conductor such as copper, the vibration of the ions increases their effective cross-section to the migrating electrons.\b [1] The higher the temperature, the more vibration, hence the greater the effective cross-section and the more collisions there are between the electrons and the ions. This reduces the mean drift velocity.\b [1] In semiconductors, thermal energy gives electrons sufficient energy to escape from their parent atoms.\b [1] The greater the temperature, the greater the\n\n8 number of electrons that can escape, so the more charge carriers there are and the lower the resistance.\b [1] ρl 1.3 × 10–⁸ × 1.5\n\n4\ta\t Resistance =  =  \b [1] A 0.008 × 10–⁶ = 2.4 Ω\b [1] A 30 × 8.0 × 10–⁹\n\nb\t l = R ρ = –₈ \b [1] 1.3 × 10 = 18.5 ≈ 18 m\b [1]\n\n5\ta\t V\t = IR = 0.48 × 5\b [1]\n\nb\t i\t Current = 0.72 – 0.48 = 0.24 A\b [1]\n\nii\t 0.24 A\b [1]\n\nc\t Resistance of the thermistor decreases\b [1]\n\nso circuit resistance decreases\b [1] so ammeter reading increases.\b [1]\n\na\t The atoms vibrate more\b [1]\n\nso their effective cross-sectional area increases and there are more collisions.\b [1]\n\nb\t Cross-sectional area\b [1]\n\nMaterial of which the wire is made\b [1] V 1.5\n\nc\t i\t R\t=  =  \b [1] I 0.24 = 6.25 Ω\b [1] ρl 1.69 × 10–⁸ × 5\n\nii\t R =  so A = \b [1] A 6.25 A = 1.35 × 10–⁸ m²\b [1] 4A d =     = 1.3 × 10–⁴ m\b [1] π\n\nd\t Less area open to air\b [1] Less cooling, therefore temperature rises.\b [1] Current decreases.\b [1] ρl ρl\n\na\t R\t=  and A = thickness × width, so t =  \b [1] A Rw 2.3 × 10³ × 36 × 10–³ t\t = ₆–₃ \